<!--
  ============================================================================
  JawaScrapfields.xml                    Jawa_Patches · authored 2026-08-13
  ============================================================================

  V1 ROW 4, override 3 of 3: the RESOURCE override.

  WHAT THE PLAYER SEES: steel slag chunks lying half buried across the open
  desert, with machine bits trodden into the sand around them. Not in ruins,
  not in a mountain, out in the open where you land. The planet reads as a
  place things have been crashing onto for a very long time, which is the
  reason a clan of scavengers is standing on it.

  ===========================================================================
  DESIGN INTENT FOLLOWED
  ===========================================================================
    design/Jawa/concept.md §1
      "A crashed Factory ship / Jawa stowaways story ... the gravship is
       SOMEONE ELSE'S crashed industrial vessel ... which Jawa scavenger
       mechanics have inherited and are repairing."

    design/Jawa/worldbuilding/desert_world_design.md:296, the §3B terrain
    treasure table, deep desert row:
      "A half-buried ANCIENT SALVAGE WRECK / crashed hulk you strip |
       components, tech, rare weapons"

  And the specific thing this file does is already an authored, v1 TAGGED row
  in the tile catalogue, which is the strongest backing any part of row 4 has:

    design/Jawa/worldbuilding/tile_augmentation_catalogue.md:105
      "| C5 | Meteoric metal field | ExtremeDesert, ZBiome_Badlands | 0.06 |
        ChunkSlagSteel + MineralRich mutator | none | FP | v1 - mutator +
        scatter |"

    design/Jawa/worldbuilding/tile_augmentation_catalogue.md:89
      "| A2 | Derelict geothermal tap - dead generator, AncientPipelineSection
        run, slag | ... | + AncientPipelineSection, ChunkSlagSteel | ... |
        v1 ... |"

  So ChunkSlagSteel as desert scatter is the design's own call, not ours. Two
  honest differences from C5, stated so nobody reads this as C5 delivered:
  C5 is a TILE augmentation at commonality 0.06 paired with the MineralRich
  mutator, i.e. a rare special tile. This is a thin, always-on scatter on the
  player map instead. C5 proper is v2 work and this does not close it.

  What is invented here is the NUMBER; see the tuning note.

  Why slag and not crates or components: slag is the cheapest possible thing
  to strew. It smelts to 15 steel (Core Various_Stone.xml:105-122) and only
  after a smelter, power and hauling, so a littered map is atmosphere with a
  work cost rather than free loot. Raising Ideology's AncientJunkClusters
  instead would have handed out sealed crates of finished gear, which trips
  concept.md §19.5 "no arms race". This does not.

  ===========================================================================
  EVERY FIELD BELOW IS COPIED FROM A SHIPPED GenStep_ScatterThings
  ===========================================================================
  Core/Defs/MapGeneration/CommonMapGenerator.xml:245-262, GenStepDef
  "AncientMiscDebris", is the donor for order, allowInWaterBiome, isJunk,
  minSpacing, countPer10kCellsRange, filthDef, filthExpandBy and filthChance.
  Ideology/Defs/MapGeneration/CommonMapGenerator.xml:78-84 is the donor for
  <thingDef> on the same genStep class.

  🔴 I WAS WRONG ABOUT <clusterSize>, AND THE REASONING IS THE PART THAT MATTERS.
  This file used to say clusterSize was "a field the engine does not read on
  this class", and the evidence given was zero grep hits across the Data tree.
  ⚠️ THAT GREP ONLY EVER PROVED NO SHIPPED DEF USES IT. It says nothing about
  whether the engine reads it. Read from the assembly instead:
  Verse.GenStep_ScatterThings carries a public int clusterSize, with private
  clusterCenter and leftInCluster and a static ClusterRadius. The engine reads
  it, and it is now set below.
  ⭐ GENERALISES: absence in Data/ is not absence in the code. A def-tree grep
  answers "does anything ship using this", never "does this work".
  (<spotMustBeStandable> stays unused — that one was never in question here.)

  ⚠️ TUNING — re-derived 2026-08-14, and the OLD SUM LEFT OUT A TERM:
  the real count is
      RoundToInt(size*size / RoundToInt(10000/value))  x  GetPlacementFactor
  and the old note omitted GetPlacementFactor entirely. With isJunk gone that
  factor is a fixed 1 (see below), so the arithmetic is honest again.

  countPer10kCellsRange 7~9 gives 44 to 56 chunks on a 250x250:
      v=7 -> 10000/7 = 1429, 62500/1429 = 44
      v=9 -> 10000/9 = 1111, 62500/1111 = 56
  clusterSize 10 turns that into 4-6 readable CLUMPS rather than 50 chunks
  spread evenly and invisibly. VISION's call and the reason for it: one clump
  reads as a crashed thing; several read as A FIELD SOMEBODY HAS BEEN WORKING
  OVER, which is the fiction we are selling. ⭐ Tune this on the screenshot,
  not on the number.

  WAS 12~20 (75-125 spread thin). Full theoretical yield if every chunk were
  smelted is now around 660 to 840 steel per map, one off and non renewing,
  gated behind a smelter and haul time. It does not create a ladder, so it
  clears concept.md §3.

  This GenStepDef does nothing on its own. Patches/JawaResource_Scrapfields.xml
  registers it into the player map generator.
  ============================================================================
-->
<Defs>

  <GenStepDef>
    <defName>Jawa_ScatterScrapfields</defName>
    <order>960</order>
    <genStep Class="GenStep_ScatterThings">
      <thingDef>ChunkSlagSteel</thingDef>
      <allowInWaterBiome>false</allowInWaterBiome>
      <!-- 🔴 isJunk REMOVED 2026-08-14 — it silently zeroed this entire step.
           GenStep_Scatterer.CalculateFinalCount multiplies the per-10k count by
           GetPlacementFactor(map), and when isJunk is true that factor is the
           PRODUCT of TileMutatorDef.junkDensityFactor over every mutator on the
           tile. Of 337 live mutators, 331 are 1 and Junkyard is 15 — but FIVE
           are ZERO: Dunes, Iceberg, VEE_DetachedIceberg, VEE_IceAndFire,
           VEE_QuicksandDunes (shipped values verified in
           Odyssey/Defs/TileMutators/TileMutators_Natural.xml:829-840).
           This campaign is a DESERT world whose landing tile carries Dunes, so
           on the CAMPAIGN map the count would be RoundToInt(n x 0) = 0 —
           a scavenger clan's scrap switched off by the terrain it lives on,
           silently, with no warning. That is the latent defect isJunk buys and
           it is why the flag is gone.
           ⭐ AND IT COSTS US NOTHING. An xref over 89,845 methods finds isJunk
           has EXACTLY ONE reader — GenStep_Scatterer.GetPlacementFactor, the
           density multiplier. It is not what keeps junk off the landing site;
           that is `nearPlayerStart` (ScatterAt IL_00bd), which we never set and
           did not touch. ⚠️ Do not record a tradeoff here — there isn't one.
           ⚠️ THIS IS NOT THE EXPLANATION FOR THE MEASURED 11. That reading was
           WITHDRAWN by OPS the same night (queue/OPS.md O15) and it was wrong:
           Jawa_StampGroundHulk is also isJunk, shares the tile, and inherits
           CalculateFinalCount unchanged — yet the hulk DID warn, and a zeroed
           count never enters the placement loop and so cannot warn. ⇒ the
           factor was NOT 0 on that quicktest tile, and scrapfields should have
           placed >=75 there. Registration is not the cause either: both defs
           are in Base_Player's genSteps in the fresh 01:20 dump.
           🔴 WHERE 64+ CHUNKS WENT IS STILL OPEN. Do not read this removal as
           the fix for it.
           WHAT DROPPING IT COSTS: Junkyard tiles no longer boost us 15x. We
           accept that — this is an always-on thin scatter by design, not a
           tile-keyed feature, and uniform is what the design asked for. The
           15x belongs to catalogue row C5, which is v2.
           ⛔ Do NOT "fix" this by patching Dunes' junkDensityFactor — that is a
           shared vanilla def and would change every junk scatterer in the game. -->
      <minSpacing>4</minSpacing>
      <countPer10kCellsRange>7~9</countPer10kCellsRange>
      <!-- 4-6 clumps of ~10 rather than ~50 chunks spread evenly. The engine
           DOES read this — see the reasoning correction in the header. -->
      <clusterSize>10</clusterSize>
      <filthDef>Filth_MachineBits</filthDef>
      <filthExpandBy>1</filthExpandBy>
      <filthChance>0.35</filthChance>
    </genStep>
  </GenStepDef>

</Defs>
